o":14.24826,"d":652,"h":"Mo-Sa 10:00-18:00","ph":"+39 081 415524"},{"n":"Intesa Sanpaolo","g":"essentials","k":"bank","la":40.840558,"lo":14.248814,"d":668,"h":"Mo-Fr 08:05-20:00; Sa 09:00-13:00","ph":"+39 081 7921111"},{"n":"ATM","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":40.840691,"lo":14.248825,"d":673},{"n":"Farmacia Augusteo","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":40.838732,"lo":14.248011,"d":676,"h":"Mo-Sa 09:00-20:30","ph":"+39 081 416105"},{"n":"Parafarmacia","g":"essentials","k":"pharmacy","la":40.843252,"lo":14.260681,"d":704},{"n":"ATM","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":40.843309,"lo":14.26087,"d":718},{"n":"Superò","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":40.844456,"lo":14.251757,"d":801,"h":"Mo-Sa 08:30-20:30; Su 08:30-14:00","w":"https://www.supero.it/","ph":"+39 081 5640087"},{"n":"Superò","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":40.832675,"lo":14.249332,"d":815},{"n":"Decò","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":40.84706,"lo":14.255812,"d":1007},{"n":"Conad","g":"essentials","k":"supermarket","la":40.847149,"lo":14.259922,"d":1069},{"n":"Pietà dei Turchini","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.841238,"lo":14.252637,"d":457,"ds":"The Church of Pietà dei Turchini is a religious building in Naples, Italy. A smaller church, located on Rua Catalana, was built originally in 1592–1595 by the Confraternity of the Incoronatella. This church was nearly destroyed by an explosion in the Castel Nuovo in 1638.","wpu":"Pietà dei Turchini","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8a/Facciata_Turchini.jpg/960px-Facciata_Turchini.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Palace of the Bank of Italy (Naples)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.8408,"lo":14.2511,"d":516,"ds":"The Palace of the Bank of Italy is a twentieth century monumental building in central Naples, Italy located in Via Cervantes.","wpu":"Palace of the Bank of Italy (Naples)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/35/Palazzo_Banca_d%27italia_Napoli.jpg/960px-Palazzo_Banca_d%27italia_Napoli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Galleria Umberto I","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.838628,"lo":14.249569,"d":545,"ds":"Galleria Umberto I is a public shopping gallery in Naples, southern Italy. It is located directly across from the San Carlo opera house. It was built between 1887 and 1890, and was the cornerstone in the decades-long rebuilding of Naples—called the risanamento (lit.","wpu":"Galleria Umberto I","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/41/Inside_of_Galleria_Umberto_I_-_Naples_2013-05-16_14-11-46_1_DxO.jpg/960px-Inside_of_Galleria_Umberto_I_-_Naples_2013-05-16_14-11-46_1_DxO.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"San Diego all'Ospedaletto, Naples","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.84236,"lo":14.25295,"d":548,"ds":"San Diego all'Ospedaletto, also known as San Giuseppe Maggiore, is a Baroque-style church located on via Medina in the rione Carità in Naples, Italy. It is located across the street from the tall modern NH Ambassador Hotel and diagonal from the Palazzo Giordano and Questura.","wpu":"San Diego all'Ospedaletto, Naples","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/80/Diego3.JPG/960px-Diego3.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Royal Palace of Naples","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.8362,"lo":14.2496,"d":574,"ds":"The Royal Palace of Naples (Italian: Palazzo Reale di Napoli) is a historic building located in Piazza del Plebiscito, in the historic center of Naples, Italy. Although the main entrance is located in this square, there are other accesses to the complex, which also includes the gardens and the Teatro di San Carlo, from the Piazza Trieste e Trento, Piazza del Municipio and Via Acton.","wpu":"Royal Palace of Naples","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/42/Napoli_2019_-Royal_Palace_-_San_Francesco_di_Paola-_by-RaBoe_074.jpg/960px-Napoli_2019_-Royal_Palace_-_San_Francesco_di_Paola-_by-RaBoe_074.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"San Pietro Martire, Naples","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.844678,"lo":14.257475,"d":752,"ds":"San Pietro Martire (Italian: \"St. Peter, the Martyr\") is a Roman Catholic church in Naples, Italy. It is located directly across from the principal building of the University of Naples on the main street, Corso Umberto corner with Via Porta di Massa, near the port area.","wpu":"San Pietro Martire, Naples","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Martire.jpg/960px-Martire.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sant'Anna di Palazzo","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.838255,"lo":14.245996,"d":842,"ds":"Sant'Anna di Palazzo (or church of the Rosario di Palazzo) is a church in the quartiere of San Ferdinando in Naples, Italy. After the victory at the Lepanto, this church and Santa Maria della Vittoria in Naples were erected and dedicated to the Madonna del Rosario, whose devotion was felt to have contributed to the success at the battle.","wpu":"Sant'Anna di Palazzo","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c6/Napoli_-_s_Anna_di_Palazzo_facciata_1060630.JPG/960px-Napoli_-_s_Anna_di_Palazzo_facciata_1060630.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Quartieri Spagnoli","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.840833,"lo":14.246389,"d":867,"ds":"Quartieri Spagnoli (Neapolitan: Quartieri Spagnuoli; lit. 'Spanish Barracks') is an area of the city of Naples, Italy. The area, encompassing c. 800,000 square metres, consists of a grid of around eighteen streets by twelve, including a population of some 14,000 inhabitants.","wpu":"Quartieri Spagnoli","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/aa/N%C3%A1poles_vista_do_alto_-_2_%283838983981%29.jpg/960px-N%C3%A1poles_vista_do_alto_-_2_%283838983981%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sant'Anna dei Lombardi","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.844816,"lo":14.250525,"d":886,"ds":"Sant'Anna dei Lombardi (Italian for 'St. Anne of the Lombards'), also known as Santa Maria di Monte Oliveto, is an ancient church and convent located in Piazza Monteoliveto in central Naples, Italy. Across Via Monteoliveto from the Fountain in the square is the Renaissance palace of Orsini di Gravina.","wpu":"Sant'Anna dei Lombardi","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/01/Lombardi.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Palazzo Orsini di Gravina","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.845237,"lo":14.251462,"d":890,"ds":"The Palazzo Orsini di Gravina is a Renaissance-style palace on number 3 Via Monteoliveto, in the San Lorenzo quarter of Rione San Giuseppe-Carità, of central Naples, Italy. Since 1940, it has housed the Faculty of Architecture of the University of Naples. It is located across the street and a few doors north of the sleek and modern Palazzo delle Poste (Post Office).","wpu":"Palazzo Orsini di Gravina","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Napoli_-_Palazzo_Gravina.jpg/960px-Napoli_-_Palazzo_Gravina.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Fountain of the Spinacorona","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.846151,"lo":14.258564,"d":931,"ds":"The Fountain of the Spinacorona, (Italian: Fontana della Spinacorona), vulgarly known as the Fontana delle Zizze (Fountain of the Breasts), is an ancient fountain in central Naples, located against a wall of the church of Santa Caterina della Spina Corona.","wpu":"Fountain of the Spinacorona","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/74/Fontana_della_Spinacorona.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Santa Maria della Catena, Naples","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.83156,"lo":14.248585,"d":949,"ds":"Santa Maria della Catena or Santa Maria del Porto is a church in Borgo Santa Lucia of Naples, Italy. The church was founded in 1576 by the inhabitants of the quartiere, and dedicated to the Madonna della Catena, an important Marian cult in Naples and Sicily. The legend holds that in 1390 in Palermo, three innocent, yet condemned, prisoners had their execution delayed due to a downpour.","wpu":"Santa Maria della Catena, Naples","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/CaternaNapoli.jpg/960px-CaternaNapoli.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Santa Maria della Concezione a Montecalvario","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.842816,"lo":14.246101,"d":990,"ds":"Santa Maria della Concezione a Montecalvario is a church in central Naples, Italy. Founded as a small church in 1579, it was attached to the adjacent monastery and school of the Concezione. It is located on via Concezione a Montecalvario #32.","wpu":"Santa Maria della Concezione a Montecalvario","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/03/InternoConcezioneM.jpg/960px-InternoConcezioneM.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Palazzo Serra di Cassano","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":40.8335,"lo":14.2458,"d":993,"ds":"Palazzo Serra di Cassano is an aristocratic palace in Naples, Italy, built for the wealthy Serra family, one of the 54 families of the 'old nobility' of Genoa, whose family was part of an albergo. The family insignia (crest) is frescoed on the ceiling of its Great Hall. The family had economic interests in banking, insurance and law.","wpu":"Palazzo Serra di Cassano","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Napoli_-_Palazzo_Serra_di_Cassano_%28ingresso%29.jpg/960px-Napoli_-_Palazzo_Serra_di_Cassano_%28ingresso%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Naples ( NAY-pəlz; Italian: Napoli [ˈnaːpoli] ; Neapolitan: Napule [ˈnɑːpələ]) is the regional capital of Campania, southern Italy. With a population of 905,050 within the city's administrative limits as of 2026, it is the largest city in southern Italy and the third-largest city in Italy after Rome and Milan, while its province-level municipality is the third most populous metropolitan city in Italy with a population of 2,954,847 residents – or the second largest according to OECD data.","wpu":"Naples","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Napoli_-_Maschio_Angioino_-_202209302342_3.jpg/960px-Napoli_-_Maschio_Angioino_-_202209302342_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}